==Translingual==

===Glyph origin===
Use as a multiplication or product operator is usually attributed to German polymath {{w|Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz}}. On 29 July 1698, he wrote in a letter to {{w|Johann Bernoulli}} (translated by {{w|Florian Cajori}}):<ref>{{cite-book|author=w:Florian Cajori|year=1928|month=September|chapter=Signs of Multiplication|title=w:A History of Mathematical Notations|volume=I (Notations in Elementary Mathematics)|location=LaSalle, Ill.|publisher=w:Open Court Publishing Company|page=267|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/b29980343_0001/page/267/mode/1up|oclc=327126|passage=The dot was introduced as a symbol for multiplication by G. W. Leibniz. On July 29, 1698, he wrote in a letter to John Bernoulli:{{nb...}}}}.</ref>
<blockquote>I do not like {{l|mul|×}} as a symbol for multiplication, as it is easily confounded with ''x;'' {{...}} often I simply relate two quantities by an interposed dot and indicate multiplication by ''ZC'' · ''LM''. Hence, in designating ratio I use not one point but two points, which I use at the same time for division.</blockquote>

===Symbol===
{{mul-symbol|name=interpunct}}

# {{lb|mul|mathematics}} {{n-g|The [[product]] [[operator]].}}
#: {{syn|mul|×|*}}
#: {{ant|mul|÷|/}}
#: {{ux|mul|7 '''·''' 8 {{=}} 56}}
# {{lb|mul|in a few countries, such as the UK}}<!--avoid placing this into the category "British Translingual"!--> {{n-g|A [[decimal point]].}}
#: {{ux|mul|3'''·'''14, £1,234'''·'''99}}
# {{lb|mul|mathematics|[[vectors]]}} {{n-g|The [[dot product]] operator.}}
#: {{ux|mul|(1,2,5) '''·''' (3,4,−1) {{=}} 6}}
# {{lb|mul|mathematics}} {{n-g|Indicates what elements can vary.}}
#: {{ux|en|If we have ''f(x,y)'' we can analyze the properties of ''f('''·''',y)'' which is a function of ''x'' found by fixing a particular y.}}
# {{lb|mul|chemistry}} {{n-g|Indicates addition compounds.}}
#: {{ux|mul|BF<sub>3</sub>'''·'''NH<sub>3</sub>}}
# {{lb|mul|NAPA}} [[vowel]] [[length]], used in the alphabets of a number of American languages (e.g. see {{angbr|[[a·]]}}). A less common variant is {{angbr|[[꞉]]}}.
# {{lb|mul|music}} {{ng|A [[pointing mark]] in [[Anglican chant]], which indicates [[nonstandard]] places in the [[text]] where the chant should change [[note]].}}
#: {{cot|mul|{{!}}|'|:|*|—|‖|†}}
# {{n-g|See '''[[・#Japanese|・]]''' for the middle dot used in [[Japanese]] [[katakana]].}}
# {{n-g|See {{m|mul|‧}} for the hyphenation point.}}

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|decimal point}} Many Britons employ the American decimal point ⟨{{l|mul|.}}⟩ owing to a general lack of support for the middot character on computers. It remains common in writing done by hand.
* {{sense|addition compound}} The IUPAC Recommendations for Nomenclature of Organic Chemistry<ref>''Nomenclature of Inorganic Chemistry – IUPAC Recommendations 2005'', 2005, p. 28, http://old.iupac.org/publications/books/rbook/Red_Book_2005.pdf</ref> state:  “Centre dots in formulae of (formal) addition compounds, including hydrates, adducts, clathrates, double salts and double oxides, separate the individual constituents. The dot is written in the centre of the line to distinguish it from a full stop (period).”

